QUIZ4 = {1: [{'q': 'A 24 VDC circuit draws 0.5 A. What is the load resistance?', 'options': ['12 Ω', '48 Ω', '24 Ω', '0.5 Ω'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'R = V/I = 24/0.5 = 48 Ω. Ohm\'s Law (V = I × R) rearranges in three ways: V = IR to find voltage drop, I = V/R to find current, R = V/I to find resistance. Always verify units: volts, amps, ohms. Double the resistance and you halve the current at the same voltage. Use this mentally when checking a 24 VDC coil: 24 V / 0.5 A = 48 Ω confirms the coil is intact.'}, {'q': 'In a series circuit, what stays the same at every component?', 'options': ['Voltage', 'Current', 'Resistance', 'Power'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'In a series circuit there is only one path for current, so every element carries the same amperage. Voltage distributes in proportion to resistance (V = IR per element). Practical implication: one open in series breaks the entire circuit. Technician trick: when a string of series loads has no voltage at the last load, measure each element\'s voltage drop to find the open &mdash; the open shows full supply voltage, all others show 0 V. This is Kirchhoff\'s Voltage Law applied to fault isolation.'}, {'q': 'What does a higher AWG number indicate about a wire?', 'options': ['Larger diameter', 'Smaller diameter', 'Higher voltage rating', 'More strands'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'American Wire Gauge (AWG) is a logarithmic inverse scale: AWG 14 is thicker than AWG 18. Every 3 gauge steps roughly halves the cross-sectional area; every 6 steps halves it by a factor of 4. NEC Table 310.16 lists ampacity per gauge (e.g., AWG 12 Cu = 20 A at 75&deg;C in conduit). Thinner wire has higher resistance per foot &mdash; it heats more at the same current and has more voltage drop over long runs. Always derate for conduit fill, ambient temperature, and continuous loads.'}], 2: [{'q': 'What is the correct order of a PLC scan cycle?', 'options': ['Solve logic → read inputs → write outputs', 'Read inputs → solve logic → write outputs', 'Write outputs → read inputs → solve logic', 'Read inputs → write outputs → solve logic'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'The PLC scan cycle has four phases: (1) Input scan &mdash; copy all physical input states into the input image table. (2) Program execution &mdash; solve rungs top-to-bottom using the frozen image, writing results to output image. (3) Output scan &mdash; copy the output image to physical outputs simultaneously. (4) Housekeeping &mdash; comms, diagnostics, watchdog reset. A typical small PLC scan takes 1&ndash;10 ms. If the watchdog expires before housekeeping, the CPU faults.'}, {'q': 'A normally-open contact in ladder logic passes power when its bit is:', 'options': ['0 (false)', '1 (true)', 'Toggling', 'Held'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'An XIC (Examine If Closed) instruction passes power-flow when the bit it references is 1 (TRUE). An XIO (Examine If Open) passes power when the bit is 0. This is NOT the same as the physical wire state &mdash; the bit is what matters. A physical NC hardwire switch mapped to a PLC input will be 1 in normal (closed) state; an XIC on that bit conducts normally and opens on fault, which is the safe-state logic pattern.'}, {'q': 'Which memory holds the real-time state of physical field wiring?', 'options': ['Data files', 'The I/O image table', 'The user program', 'Retentive memory'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'The input image table (I:x.x) is a memory buffer that captures every physical input state at the START of the scan. The PLC program reads these frozen values &mdash; not live wire states &mdash; so logic stays consistent for one full cycle. At scan end, the output image table (O:x.x) writes accumulated results back to real outputs simultaneously. This scan-synchronous model prevents mid-scan glitches and is why a very short pulse may be missed if shorter than one scan time.'}], 4: [{'q': 'Which sensor detects only metallic targets at short range?', 'options': ['Capacitive', 'Inductive proximity', 'Photoelectric', 'Ultrasonic'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'An inductive prox contains an LC oscillator. When a ferrous (or non-ferrous metal) target enters the sensing field, eddy currents induced in the target absorb energy, damping the oscillation amplitude. The sensor\'s circuitry detects this drop in oscillation and switches the output. Range depends on target material: rated range is for mild steel; aluminum/brass detect at roughly 0.5&times; range. Inductive prox sensors are sealed, vibration-proof, and immune to dirt &mdash; ideal for harsh conveyor environments.'}, {'q': 'A PNP (sourcing) discrete sensor supplies which signal when active?', 'options': ['Ground/0 V to the load', 'Positive V to the load', 'An analog current', 'A pulse train'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'PNP (sourcing) sensors push +VDC on their output when active &mdash; the load connects between output and 0 V. NPN (sinking) sensors pull the output to 0 V when active &mdash; the load connects between +VDC and the output. Most PLCs accept either type; wire PNP to a current-sourcing input module or NPN to a current-sinking module. Mixing types on the same common can cause erratic behavior. The sensor body often shows a wiring diagram on the label.'}, {'q': 'A retro-reflective photoeye uses a reflector and detects a part when the beam is:', 'options': ['Reflected back', 'Broken/blocked', 'Doubled', 'Inverted'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'A retro-reflective photoeye sends a beam to a corner-cube reflector and monitors the return signal. When a target passes between the sensor and reflector, it blocks the beam &mdash; the receiver goes dark and triggers output. Advantage over diffuse: far longer range and immune to target surface color/reflectivity. Gotcha: shiny targets can act as their own reflector and miss-detect; use polarized lenses or switch to diffuse mode.'}], 5: [{'q': 'To maintain constant motor torque, a VFD holds which ratio roughly constant?', 'options': ['Current/frequency', 'Volts/Hertz', 'Power/speed', 'Slip/torque'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'An induction motor develops torque through magnetic flux, which depends on the voltage-to-frequency ratio. If a VFD lowers frequency without also lowering voltage, flux saturates the core and current spikes; if voltage drops faster than frequency, flux weakens and the motor loses torque capacity. By holding V/Hz constant from ~5 Hz up to base speed, the drive maintains rated flux and torque. Above base speed (field-weakening region), voltage is capped at rated and torque falls off.'}, {'q': 'What does a VFD DC bus do?', 'options': ['Rectifies then filters AC into DC before the inverter', 'Steps up line voltage', 'Measures motor speed', 'Brakes the motor mechanically'], 'answer': 0, 'explain': 'The rectifier + capacitor bus creates a smooth DC link the inverter switches into variable-frequency AC.'}, {'q': 'Motor slip is the difference between:', 'options': ['Line and load voltage', 'Synchronous and actual rotor speed', 'Start and run current', 'Stator and rotor resistance'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Synchronous speed Ns = 120 × f / p (f = frequency, p = poles). An induction motor rotor always runs slightly slower than Ns &mdash; this difference is slip. Slip is what creates the relative motion between rotor bars and stator field, inducing rotor current and thus torque. At no load, slip is near zero (maybe 0.5%). At full rated load, slip is typically 2&ndash;5%. Excessive slip at rated load indicates a high-resistance rotor or degraded motor condition.'}], 11: [{'q': 'On the hierarchy of controls, which is the MOST effective?', 'options': ['PPE', 'Administrative controls', 'Elimination / design-out the hazard', 'Guarding'], 'answer': 2, 'explain': 'NIOSH hierarchy of controls ranks methods from most to least effective: 1. Elimination (remove the hazard entirely). 2. Substitution (replace with a safer process). 3. Engineering controls (machine guards, interlocks, ventilation). 4. Administrative controls (procedures, training, scheduling). 5. PPE (last resort &mdash; relies on human compliance). PPE is never the primary control; it protects only when higher-level controls have already reduced risk.'}, {'q': 'A Category 0 stop per IEC 60204-1 removes power:', 'options': ['After a controlled ramp', 'Immediately/uncontrolled', 'Only to the HMI', 'After a timer'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'IEC 60204-1 defines three stop categories. Category 0: immediately remove power from actuators &mdash; uncontrolled coast-to-stop. Fastest halt, but can leave a conveyor in mid-position or cause mechanical shock. Category 1: controlled deceleration to stop, then power removed. Preferred for high-inertia loads. Category 2: controlled stop with power maintained after stop (servo hold). E-stop circuits are usually Cat 0 or Cat 1 depending on the hazard analysis per ISO 13849.'}, {'q': 'Lockout/tagout primarily protects against:', 'options': ['Arc flash only', 'Unexpected energization during service', 'Overcurrent', 'Ground faults'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Lockout/Tagout (LOTO) follows OSHA 29 CFR 1910.147. The authorized employee: (1) notifies affected employees, (2) shuts down equipment normally, (3) isolates all energy sources (electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, gravity), (4) applies a personal lock and tag, (5) releases stored energy (bleed pressure, block gravity loads), (6) verifies zero energy before touching the work zone. Each worker adds their own lock &mdash; equipment cannot be re-energized until every lock is removed.'}], 13: [{'q': 'A PLC timer (TON) times while its rung is:', 'options': ['False', 'True/enabled', 'Toggling', 'Reset'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'TON (Timer ON Delay): while the rung is true (enabled), the ACC value increments each time base. When ACC ≥ PRE, the DN (Done) bit sets. If the rung goes false before DN, ACC resets to 0. Use case: delay starting a conveyor 2 seconds after a permissive to let downstream equipment stabilize. Compare with TOF (Timer OFF Delay) where the timer runs after the rung goes false, useful for extended lube after a motor stops. ACC is visible live in RSLogix/Studio 5000 for diagnostics.'}, {'q': 'Which instruction is best for indexing through an array of recipe values?', 'options': ['A one-shot', 'Indirect addressing / indexed pointer', 'A latch', 'A JSR with no args'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Indexed addressing uses a pointer register (e.g., N7:0 in SLC, or an index offset in Studio 5000) to reference array elements without hard-coding each subscript. Example: MOVE N7[Idx] to Dest, then increment Idx to process the next recipe step. This pattern is essential for recipe-driven systems, batch sequencers, and loop tuning tables where 10&ndash;100 sets of parameters must be loaded without duplicating rungs. Always validate that Idx never exceeds the array dimension to prevent a major fault.'}, {'q': 'Structured routines/subroutines mainly improve:', 'options': ['Scan speed only', 'Code organization and reuse', 'I/O count', 'Analog resolution'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Breaking a PLC program into named subroutines (one per machine function: Conveyor, Divert, Safety) isolates logic so a technician can jump directly to the relevant routine. Each routine can be tested independently in offline simulation before integration. When the same function appears on multiple lines, one shared routine (or AOI) means one place to update. IEC 61131-3 formalizes this as Program Organization Units (POUs): Programs, Function Blocks, and Functions.'}], 16: [{'q': 'MTBF stands for:', 'options': ['Mean Time Between Failures', 'Maximum Time Before Fault', 'Mean Test Bench Frequency', 'Minimum Time By Failure'], 'answer': 0, 'explain': 'Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) = total operating time ÷ number of failures during that period. A pump that ran 10,000 hours and failed 5 times has MTBF = 2,000 hours. Use MTBF to set preventive maintenance intervals (typically 50&ndash;80% of MTBF) and to compare vendors. MTBF assumes exponential failure distribution (constant hazard rate) &mdash; the bathtub curve reminds us failure rates are higher early (infant mortality) and late (wear-out), so real scheduling accounts for both.'}, {'q': 'Vibration analysis and thermography are examples of which strategy?', 'options': ['Reactive maintenance', 'Predictive (condition-based) maintenance', 'Run-to-failure', 'Design maintenance'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Predictive maintenance (PdM) uses condition-monitoring data &mdash; vibration spectra, infrared thermography, oil analysis, ultrasound &mdash; to detect degradation trends before failure occurs. Unlike preventive (time-based) tasks, PdM only triggers work when data says a component is actually deteriorating, reducing unnecessary parts replacement and unplanned downtime. A bearing vibration trending upward week-over-week gives the crew time to schedule a planned outage.'}, {'q': 'Which is a PREVENTIVE (time-based) task rather than predictive?', 'options': ['Greasing bearings on a fixed schedule', 'Trending motor current', 'Oil particle analysis', 'Acoustic leak detection'], 'answer': 0, 'explain': 'Preventive maintenance (PM) schedules tasks by time or usage intervals regardless of actual equipment condition. Greasing a bearing every 500 hours or replacing a filter monthly are textbook PM examples. PM prevents failures by refreshing consumables before they degrade to failure &mdash; but it can waste resources when done too early and still miss failures if the interval is too long. Combining PM (for low-cost consumables) with PdM (for expensive components) optimizes maintenance spend.'}], 17: [{'q': 'Wire duct/wireway inside a panel is used primarily to:', 'options': ['Dissipate heat', 'Route and protect conductors neatly', 'Ground the enclosure', 'Mount breakers'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Wire duct (wireway) routes and bundles conductors inside a panel, keeping them away from heat sources and sharp edges. The slotted sides allow cables to exit cleanly toward terminals without sharp bends. NEC and UL 508A specify fill ratios &mdash; typically 40% of duct cross-sectional area for control wiring &mdash; to prevent overheating and allow future additions. Color-coding duct (gray for control, yellow for safety, orange for DC) speeds troubleshooting significantly.'}, {'q': 'A NEMA 12 enclosure is rated for:', 'options': ['Submersion', 'Dust and dripping liquids (indoor)', 'Explosive atmospheres', 'Outdoor rain only'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'NEMA enclosure ratings define protection against environmental ingress. NEMA 12 (indoor): protects against circulating dust, falling dirt, and dripping non-corrosive liquids. NEMA 4 adds water splashing from any direction and is used near washdown areas. NEMA 4X adds corrosion resistance (stainless or fiberglass) for food/chemical environments. NEMA 7/9 are explosion-proof for hazardous locations (Class I/II, Div 1). IEC equivalent: IP54 ≈ NEMA 12; IP65 ≈ NEMA 4; IP66 ≈ NEMA 4X.'}, {'q': 'Why derate panel components for ambient temperature?', 'options': ['To save cost', 'Heat reduces current-carrying capacity and lifespan', 'To meet color codes', 'To increase voltage'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Most electrical components are rated at 40°C ambient. Enclosure internal temperature can be 10&ndash;20°C above ambient due to self-heating. At 50°C, a wire rated 20 A at 40°C may derate to ~16 A per NEC correction factors. Contactors, VFDs, and PLCs also publish derating curves &mdash; exceeding them shortens insulation life exponentially (Arrhenius: every 10°C rise roughly halves insulation life). Solutions: ventilation fans, air conditioning, or selecting components rated for higher ambient temperatures.'}]}
